1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,694
If you were born somewhere around nineteen

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:05,068
forty-eight, I want you to close your

3
00:00:05,068 --> 00:00:06,222
eyes for a moment.

4
00:00:06,402 --> 00:00:09,194
It's a cool September morning in nineteen

5
00:00:09,194 --> 00:00:09,943
fifty-five.

6
00:00:10,123 --> 00:00:11,575
You're seven years old.

7
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:13,988
The screen door slams behind you,

8
00:00:13,988 --> 00:00:16,289
and your mother calls out from the

9
00:00:16,289 --> 00:00:18,590
kitchen, telling you not to let it

10
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:20,755
slam, the way she has a thousand

11
00:00:20,755 --> 00:00:21,635
times before.

12
00:00:21,984 --> 00:00:23,303
Your shoes are new,

13
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:24,621
a little too stiff,

14
00:00:24,621 --> 00:00:26,980
bought one size big so you'll grow

15
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:27,673
into them.

16
00:00:27,853 --> 00:00:29,686
And leaning against the porch,

17
00:00:29,686 --> 00:00:31,947
red paint shining in the early light,

18
00:00:31,947 --> 00:00:34,086
is the thing you have dreamed about

19
00:00:34,086 --> 00:00:35,063
all summer long.

20
00:00:35,243 --> 00:00:37,101
Your very first bicycle.

21
00:00:37,450 --> 00:00:38,634
You don't know it yet,

22
00:00:38,634 --> 00:00:40,409
but you are standing at the start

23
00:00:40,409 --> 00:00:41,700
of a remarkable journey.

24
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:43,675
In the next nine years you'll see

25
00:00:43,675 --> 00:00:44,817
rock and roll arrive.

26
00:00:44,997 --> 00:00:47,244
You'll see a little silver ball go

27
00:00:47,244 --> 00:00:49,955
up into space and frighten the grown-ups.

28
00:00:50,135 --> 00:00:52,307
You'll learn to hide under a school

29
00:00:52,307 --> 00:00:53,611
desk, learn to dance,

30
00:00:53,611 --> 00:00:54,790
and learn to drive.

31
00:00:54,970 --> 00:00:55,801
And one day,

32
00:00:55,801 --> 00:00:57,880
with money you saved yourself,

33
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:59,958
you'll turn a key in an engine

34
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:01,344
that belongs to you.

35
00:01:01,693 --> 00:01:03,452
From your first bicycle,

36
00:01:03,452 --> 00:01:04,770
to your first car.

37
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:06,707
This is what it was like to

38
00:01:06,707 --> 00:01:09,246
grow up in nineteen fifty-five America.

39
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:11,879
So pour yourself a cup of coffee,

40
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:14,480
get comfortable, and let's go back,

41
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:15,521
one more time.

42
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:18,396
Let's start where every day started and

43
00:01:18,396 --> 00:01:18,785
ended.

44
00:01:18,965 --> 00:01:19,557
At home.

45
00:01:19,737 --> 00:01:22,226
If your family was like millions of

46
00:01:22,226 --> 00:01:24,359
others in nineteen fifty-five,

47
00:01:24,359 --> 00:01:26,563
you might have been living in a

48
00:01:26,563 --> 00:01:27,701
brand-new house.

49
00:01:27,881 --> 00:01:28,973
After the war,

50
00:01:28,973 --> 00:01:31,470
America built homes at a pace it

51
00:01:31,470 --> 00:01:33,187
had never seen before.

52
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:35,140
Places like Levittown,

53
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,671
out on Long Island,

54
00:01:36,671 --> 00:01:40,862
turned potato fields into whole neighborhoods almost

55
00:01:40,862 --> 00:01:41,668
overnight.

56
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:43,724
The houses looked alike.

57
00:01:43,904 --> 00:01:45,727
Two or three bedrooms.

58
00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:48,466
One bathroom, which the whole family shared,

59
00:01:48,466 --> 00:01:50,269
and which you learned to get in

60
00:01:50,269 --> 00:01:52,246
and out of quickly in the morning.

61
00:01:52,426 --> 00:01:54,710
A picture window in the front room,

62
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:56,798
so everybody could see the lawn,

63
00:01:56,798 --> 00:01:58,951
and the lawn could see everybody.

64
00:01:59,131 --> 00:02:00,511
With a G I Bill loan,

65
00:02:00,511 --> 00:02:02,944
a young veteran could move his family

66
00:02:02,944 --> 00:02:04,982
in with very little money down.

67
00:02:05,332 --> 00:02:06,226
And the kitchen.

68
00:02:06,406 --> 00:02:07,881
That was the heart of it all.

69
00:02:08,061 --> 00:02:11,036
Maybe your mother's kitchen was painted a

70
00:02:11,036 --> 00:02:13,431
soft turquoise, or butter yellow,

71
00:02:13,431 --> 00:02:14,446
or mint green.

72
00:02:14,626 --> 00:02:16,924
There was a chrome-edged table with a

73
00:02:16,924 --> 00:02:19,408
Formica top, and vinyl chairs that stuck

74
00:02:19,408 --> 00:02:21,146
to the backs of your legs in

75
00:02:21,146 --> 00:02:21,581
summer.

76
00:02:21,761 --> 00:02:24,450
The refrigerator had rounded corners and a

77
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,242
handle you had to pull hard.

78
00:02:26,592 --> 00:02:28,452
If your family was doing well,

79
00:02:28,452 --> 00:02:30,620
there might be an electric mixer on

80
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:33,037
the counter, and a pressure cooker that

81
00:02:33,037 --> 00:02:35,268
hissed and rattled and made you just

82
00:02:35,268 --> 00:02:36,322
a little nervous.

83
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:38,173
Monday was wash day.

84
00:02:38,353 --> 00:02:41,283
The wringer washer churned in the basement,

85
00:02:41,283 --> 00:02:43,327
and the sheets went out on the

86
00:02:43,327 --> 00:02:44,486
line in the yard,

87
00:02:44,486 --> 00:02:47,075
snapping in the wind and smelling like

88
00:02:47,075 --> 00:02:49,255
sunshine when they came back in.

89
00:02:49,605 --> 00:02:51,440
Dinner was at six o'clock.

90
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:52,839
Not five-thirty.

91
00:02:53,019 --> 00:02:54,668
Not whenever you felt like it.

92
00:02:54,848 --> 00:02:56,148
Six o'clock.

93
00:02:56,328 --> 00:02:57,954
Your father came home,

94
00:02:57,954 --> 00:02:59,136
hung up his hat,

95
00:02:59,136 --> 00:03:01,576
and the family sat down together.

96
00:03:01,756 --> 00:03:03,067
Meatloaf on Monday.

97
00:03:03,247 --> 00:03:05,168
Maybe fish sticks on Friday,

98
00:03:05,168 --> 00:03:07,775
because those had only just arrived in

99
00:03:07,775 --> 00:03:10,314
the grocery freezer a couple of years

100
00:03:10,314 --> 00:03:10,794
before.

101
00:03:11,143 --> 00:03:13,901
There was Jell-O salad with something suspended

102
00:03:13,901 --> 00:03:15,955
inside it that you were never quite

103
00:03:15,955 --> 00:03:16,600
sure about.

104
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:18,659
And you did not leave the table

105
00:03:18,659 --> 00:03:20,296
until your plate was clean,

106
00:03:20,296 --> 00:03:23,327
because there were children going hungry somewhere

107
00:03:23,327 --> 00:03:24,115
in the world,

108
00:03:24,115 --> 00:03:26,177
and your mother made sure you knew

109
00:03:26,177 --> 00:03:26,358
it.

110
00:03:26,708 --> 00:03:28,705
The milkman came to the house.

111
00:03:28,885 --> 00:03:29,942
Think about that.

112
00:03:30,122 --> 00:03:32,283
A man in a white uniform brought

113
00:03:32,283 --> 00:03:34,444
glass bottles to your back step,

114
00:03:34,444 --> 00:03:36,403
and took the empty ones away.

115
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:38,832
The bread man came too,

116
00:03:38,832 --> 00:03:39,810
sometimes.

117
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:40,967
And the doctor,

118
00:03:40,967 --> 00:03:42,528
if you were sick enough,

119
00:03:42,528 --> 00:03:44,806
still made house calls with a black

120
00:03:44,806 --> 00:03:45,586
leather bag.

121
00:03:45,936 --> 00:03:47,457
Then there was the television.

122
00:03:47,637 --> 00:03:50,187
By the middle of the nineteen fifties,

123
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:52,737
around two out of every three American

124
00:03:52,737 --> 00:03:53,810
homes had a set,

125
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:56,427
according to the industry counts of the

126
00:03:56,427 --> 00:03:56,763
time.

127
00:03:56,943 --> 00:03:58,758
Just a few years earlier,

128
00:03:58,758 --> 00:04:00,646
it had been almost nobody.

129
00:04:00,826 --> 00:04:02,422
If your family had one,

130
00:04:02,422 --> 00:04:04,641
it was a big wooden cabinet with

131
00:04:04,641 --> 00:04:06,306
a small, rounded screen,

132
00:04:06,306 --> 00:04:08,802
and the picture was black and white.

133
00:04:09,152 --> 00:04:11,369
Sometimes the picture rolled,

134
00:04:11,369 --> 00:04:13,814
and your father would get up and

135
00:04:13,814 --> 00:04:15,495
fiddle with the knobs,

136
00:04:15,495 --> 00:04:17,482
or climb up on the roof to

137
00:04:17,482 --> 00:04:21,074
turn the antenna while somebody inside shouted,

138
00:04:21,074 --> 00:04:22,450
better, no, worse,

139
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:23,061
go back.

140
00:04:23,241 --> 00:04:25,633
There were only a handful of channels.

141
00:04:25,813 --> 00:04:27,812
When the broadcast day ended,

142
00:04:27,812 --> 00:04:28,778
late at night,

143
00:04:28,778 --> 00:04:31,467
the station played the national anthem.

144
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:33,881
Then the screen turned to a test

145
00:04:33,881 --> 00:04:36,139
pattern, humming at you in the dark

146
00:04:36,139 --> 00:04:36,913
living room.

147
00:04:37,093 --> 00:04:39,237
And you weren't supposed to sit too

148
00:04:39,237 --> 00:04:39,973
close to it.

149
00:04:40,153 --> 00:04:42,829
Everybody's mother said it would ruin your

150
00:04:42,829 --> 00:04:43,148
eyes.

151
00:04:43,497 --> 00:04:44,970
On Saturday mornings,

152
00:04:44,970 --> 00:04:47,495
your mother went to the supermarket,

153
00:04:47,495 --> 00:04:49,599
a big bright new kind of store

154
00:04:49,599 --> 00:04:52,124
with wide aisles and shopping carts.

155
00:04:52,304 --> 00:04:53,312
At the checkout,

156
00:04:53,312 --> 00:04:55,768
the cashier handed her little sheets of

157
00:04:55,768 --> 00:04:58,162
trading stamps, and at home you licked

158
00:04:58,162 --> 00:05:00,493
them and pasted them into booklets at

159
00:05:00,493 --> 00:05:01,626
the kitchen table.

160
00:05:01,806 --> 00:05:04,784
Enough books, and you could trade them

161
00:05:04,784 --> 00:05:06,117
in for a toaster,

162
00:05:06,117 --> 00:05:06,900
or a lamp,

163
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:08,389
or a set of dishes.

164
00:05:08,569 --> 00:05:10,822
It felt like getting something for free.

165
00:05:11,171 --> 00:05:13,206
Now it's eight o'clock in the morning,

166
00:05:13,206 --> 00:05:14,759
and you're walking to school.

167
00:05:14,939 --> 00:05:16,367
Yes, walking.

168
00:05:16,547 --> 00:05:18,013
Most kids walked,

169
00:05:18,013 --> 00:05:19,737
or rode their bikes,

170
00:05:19,737 --> 00:05:21,893
sometimes a mile or more,

171
00:05:21,893 --> 00:05:24,825
and nobody thought twice about it.

172
00:05:25,005 --> 00:05:26,688
You walked with your brother,

173
00:05:26,688 --> 00:05:28,546
or the kids from down the block.

174
00:05:28,726 --> 00:05:30,084
If it was raining,

175
00:05:30,084 --> 00:05:30,990
you got wet.

176
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:32,318
If it was snowing,

177
00:05:32,318 --> 00:05:34,039
you got a story to tell for

178
00:05:34,039 --> 00:05:35,442
the rest of your life.

179
00:05:35,792 --> 00:05:37,813
In your hand was your lunchbox.

180
00:05:37,993 --> 00:05:38,765
A metal one,

181
00:05:38,765 --> 00:05:39,924
if you were lucky,

182
00:05:39,924 --> 00:05:41,661
with a picture on the side.

183
00:05:41,841 --> 00:05:43,548
A cowboy hero,

184
00:05:43,548 --> 00:05:44,279
maybe.

185
00:05:44,459 --> 00:05:46,634
And in nineteen fifty-five,

186
00:05:46,634 --> 00:05:48,969
quite possibly Davy Crockett,

187
00:05:48,969 --> 00:05:52,028
because every kid in America was crazy

188
00:05:52,028 --> 00:05:54,444
about Davy Crockett that year.

189
00:05:54,624 --> 00:05:57,154
Inside was a sandwich wrapped in wax

190
00:05:57,154 --> 00:05:59,614
paper, and a little thermos of milk

191
00:05:59,614 --> 00:06:02,075
that was never quite cold enough by

192
00:06:02,075 --> 00:06:02,426
noon.

193
00:06:02,775 --> 00:06:05,909
Your classroom had tall windows and wooden

194
00:06:05,909 --> 00:06:07,327
desks in neat rows.

195
00:06:07,507 --> 00:06:09,728
Some still had an inkwell hole in

196
00:06:09,728 --> 00:06:12,420
the corner, left over from your parents'

197
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:13,161
generation.

198
00:06:13,341 --> 00:06:15,207
At the front of the room hung

199
00:06:15,207 --> 00:06:16,301
an American flag,

200
00:06:16,301 --> 00:06:19,068
and a portrait of George Washington looking

201
00:06:19,068 --> 00:06:20,806
down at you very seriously.

202
00:06:21,155 --> 00:06:23,048
Every morning began the same way.

203
00:06:23,228 --> 00:06:24,030
You stood up,

204
00:06:24,030 --> 00:06:25,880
put your hand over your heart,

205
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:27,976
and said the Pledge of Allegiance.

206
00:06:28,156 --> 00:06:29,625
Just the year before,

207
00:06:29,625 --> 00:06:31,235
in nineteen fifty-four,

208
00:06:31,235 --> 00:06:33,403
two new words had been added to

209
00:06:33,403 --> 00:06:33,613
it.

210
00:06:33,793 --> 00:06:34,664
Under God.

211
00:06:34,844 --> 00:06:36,876
Your older cousins had learned it one

212
00:06:36,876 --> 00:06:37,096
way.

213
00:06:37,276 --> 00:06:38,635
You learned it the new way.

214
00:06:38,984 --> 00:06:40,920
You learned to read with Dick and

215
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:41,214
Jane.

216
00:06:41,394 --> 00:06:42,520
See Spot run.

217
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:45,033
Run, Spot, run.

218
00:06:45,213 --> 00:06:47,970
You practiced penmanship over and over,

219
00:06:47,970 --> 00:06:50,798
the loops and curves of cursive writing,

220
00:06:50,798 --> 00:06:52,353
until your hand ached.

221
00:06:52,533 --> 00:06:54,684
The teacher was an authority you didn't

222
00:06:54,684 --> 00:06:55,181
question.

223
00:06:55,361 --> 00:06:57,268
And if you got in trouble at

224
00:06:57,268 --> 00:06:59,652
school, you got in trouble again at

225
00:06:59,652 --> 00:06:59,993
home.

226
00:07:00,342 --> 00:07:02,856
At midmorning, a little carton of milk

227
00:07:02,856 --> 00:07:04,377
appeared on every desk,

228
00:07:04,377 --> 00:07:06,494
and you drank it through a paper

229
00:07:06,494 --> 00:07:06,890
straw.

230
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:09,512
Every week, the Weekly Reader arrived,

231
00:07:09,512 --> 00:07:11,504
a thin newspaper just for kids,

232
00:07:11,504 --> 00:07:14,524
telling you about rockets and faraway countries

233
00:07:14,524 --> 00:07:16,451
and the wonders of the future.

234
00:07:16,631 --> 00:07:18,168
And four times a year,

235
00:07:18,168 --> 00:07:20,473
you carried home a report card in

236
00:07:20,473 --> 00:07:22,080
a stiff paper envelope,

237
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:24,106
and handed it to your father,

238
00:07:24,106 --> 00:07:24,874
and waited.

239
00:07:25,224 --> 00:07:26,769
And then there were the drills.

240
00:07:26,949 --> 00:07:27,949
Every so often,

241
00:07:27,949 --> 00:07:30,883
without warning, the teacher would call out,

242
00:07:30,883 --> 00:07:33,084
and the whole class would drop to

243
00:07:33,084 --> 00:07:35,618
the floor and crawl under their desks,

244
00:07:35,618 --> 00:07:38,019
hands folded over the backs of their

245
00:07:38,019 --> 00:07:38,419
necks.

246
00:07:38,599 --> 00:07:40,027
Duck and cover.

247
00:07:40,207 --> 00:07:41,436
You'd seen the film,

248
00:07:41,436 --> 00:07:42,727
with Bert the Turtle,

249
00:07:42,727 --> 00:07:44,817
who pulled his head into his shell

250
00:07:44,817 --> 00:07:45,861
when danger came.

251
00:07:46,211 --> 00:07:47,902
You did it the way children do

252
00:07:47,902 --> 00:07:48,522
everything.

253
00:07:48,702 --> 00:07:50,873
Half seriously, half giggling.

254
00:07:51,053 --> 00:07:52,663
But somewhere deep down,

255
00:07:52,663 --> 00:07:55,012
a little seed of worry got planted.

256
00:07:55,192 --> 00:07:57,316
Your generation was among the first to

257
00:07:57,316 --> 00:07:59,383
grow up knowing the whole world could

258
00:07:59,383 --> 00:08:00,892
end on an ordinary Tuesday.

259
00:08:01,072 --> 00:08:02,257
You carried that,

260
00:08:02,257 --> 00:08:03,998
quietly, for a long time.

261
00:08:04,348 --> 00:08:05,843
At recess, though,

262
00:08:05,843 --> 00:08:07,669
the worry disappeared.

263
00:08:07,849 --> 00:08:10,137
There was kickball and tag and jump

264
00:08:10,137 --> 00:08:12,818
rope, and the girls chanting rhymes while

265
00:08:12,818 --> 00:08:14,780
the rope slapped the blacktop.

266
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:16,341
There were marbles,

267
00:08:16,341 --> 00:08:17,650
and trading cards,

268
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:20,340
and whoever was the fastest runner in

269
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:21,067
the class.

270
00:08:21,247 --> 00:08:23,359
You always remember who that was,

271
00:08:23,359 --> 00:08:23,998
don't you?

272
00:08:24,178 --> 00:08:25,038
Even now.

273
00:08:25,387 --> 00:08:27,037
When the last bell rang,

274
00:08:27,037 --> 00:08:28,000
you were free.

275
00:08:28,180 --> 00:08:29,874
You dropped your books at home,

276
00:08:29,874 --> 00:08:31,951
grabbed a cookie if your mother wasn't

277
00:08:31,951 --> 00:08:33,427
looking, and you were gone.

278
00:08:33,607 --> 00:08:35,000
There was one rule,

279
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,173
really just one.

280
00:08:36,353 --> 00:08:38,652
Be home when the streetlights come on.

281
00:08:38,832 --> 00:08:41,652
Until then, the whole neighborhood belonged to

282
00:08:41,652 --> 00:08:41,897
you.

283
00:08:42,246 --> 00:08:43,768
And now, at last,

284
00:08:43,768 --> 00:08:46,542
that bicycle came into its own.

285
00:08:46,722 --> 00:08:48,398
Maybe it was a Schwinn,

286
00:08:48,398 --> 00:08:49,564
heavy and solid,

287
00:08:49,564 --> 00:08:52,260
with balloon tires and a chrome bell.

288
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:54,490
You clipped a playing card to the

289
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:57,162
fork with one of your mother's clothespins,

290
00:08:57,162 --> 00:08:59,523
so it clattered against the spokes and

291
00:08:59,523 --> 00:09:01,076
sounded, at least to you,

292
00:09:01,076 --> 00:09:02,692
exactly like a motorcycle.

293
00:09:03,041 --> 00:09:05,259
You learned to ride with no hands.

294
00:09:05,439 --> 00:09:07,215
You learned to skid in the gravel.

295
00:09:07,395 --> 00:09:09,501
You learned what it felt like to

296
00:09:09,501 --> 00:09:10,356
fall, get up,

297
00:09:10,356 --> 00:09:12,790
and not tell anybody about the scrape

298
00:09:12,790 --> 00:09:14,501
on your knee until dinner.

299
00:09:14,681 --> 00:09:16,329
That bicycle was freedom.

300
00:09:16,679 --> 00:09:18,412
It took you to the creek,

301
00:09:18,412 --> 00:09:19,659
to the vacant lot,

302
00:09:19,659 --> 00:09:21,322
and to the corner store,

303
00:09:21,322 --> 00:09:23,955
where a bottle of Coca-Cola still cost

304
00:09:23,955 --> 00:09:24,995
about a nickel,

305
00:09:24,995 --> 00:09:26,935
and a candy bar cost about a

306
00:09:26,935 --> 00:09:27,697
nickel too.

307
00:09:27,877 --> 00:09:29,712
A comic book was a dime.

308
00:09:29,892 --> 00:09:32,087
If you found an empty soda bottle

309
00:09:32,087 --> 00:09:32,819
in a ditch,

310
00:09:32,819 --> 00:09:34,947
you could return it for a couple

311
00:09:34,947 --> 00:09:35,546
of cents.

312
00:09:35,726 --> 00:09:38,355
Kids in your neighborhood knew where every

313
00:09:38,355 --> 00:09:40,358
empty bottle in town was hiding.

314
00:09:40,708 --> 00:09:42,407
In nineteen fifty-five,

315
00:09:42,407 --> 00:09:44,993
it seemed like every boy in America

316
00:09:44,993 --> 00:09:46,618
wanted a coonskin cap.

317
00:09:46,798 --> 00:09:49,297
Walt Disney's Davy Crockett had been on

318
00:09:49,297 --> 00:09:50,772
television that winter,

319
00:09:50,772 --> 00:09:53,399
and suddenly the stores couldn't keep the

320
00:09:53,399 --> 00:09:54,681
caps on the shelves.

321
00:09:54,861 --> 00:09:56,521
Boys wore them in July.

322
00:09:56,701 --> 00:09:58,176
Boys wore them to bed.

323
00:09:58,356 --> 00:10:00,591
You sang the Davy Crockett song until

324
00:10:00,591 --> 00:10:02,524
your parents begged you to stop.

325
00:10:02,873 --> 00:10:05,257
You played cowboys with cap guns that

326
00:10:05,257 --> 00:10:06,867
smelled like burnt paper.

327
00:10:07,047 --> 00:10:08,800
You played army in the woods.

328
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:11,094
You built forts out of old boards

329
00:10:11,094 --> 00:10:13,465
and blankets, and you made the rules,

330
00:10:13,465 --> 00:10:15,707
and you settled your own arguments,

331
00:10:15,707 --> 00:10:18,334
because there were no grown-ups around to

332
00:10:18,334 --> 00:10:19,615
settle them for you.

333
00:10:19,965 --> 00:10:22,985
The girls played house with baby dolls,

334
00:10:22,985 --> 00:10:25,231
and jacks on the front steps,

335
00:10:25,231 --> 00:10:27,941
and hopscotch drawn in chalk on the

336
00:10:27,941 --> 00:10:28,638
sidewalk.

337
00:10:28,818 --> 00:10:31,949
Everybody roller-skated on metal skates that clamped

338
00:10:31,949 --> 00:10:33,997
to your shoes and tightened with a

339
00:10:33,997 --> 00:10:35,442
key, a key you wore on a

340
00:10:35,442 --> 00:10:36,887
string around your neck.

341
00:10:37,237 --> 00:10:38,496
A few years later,

342
00:10:38,496 --> 00:10:40,244
the toys got even better.

343
00:10:40,424 --> 00:10:42,156
In nineteen fifty-eight,

344
00:10:42,156 --> 00:10:45,115
a company called Wham-O started selling a

345
00:10:45,115 --> 00:10:48,073
simple plastic ring called the Hula Hoop,

346
00:10:48,073 --> 00:10:50,310
and millions sold in just a few

347
00:10:50,310 --> 00:10:50,815
months.

348
00:10:50,995 --> 00:10:52,354
Then came the Frisbee.

349
00:10:52,534 --> 00:10:55,288
And then, in nineteen fifty-nine,

350
00:10:55,288 --> 00:10:56,957
a doll named Barbie.

351
00:10:57,307 --> 00:10:59,227
But in nineteen fifty-five,

352
00:10:59,227 --> 00:11:00,721
you didn't need much.

353
00:11:00,901 --> 00:11:02,155
You had a bicycle,

354
00:11:02,155 --> 00:11:03,130
a few friends,

355
00:11:03,130 --> 00:11:05,011
and a whole long afternoon.

356
00:11:05,191 --> 00:11:07,246
And when the sky turned orange,

357
00:11:07,246 --> 00:11:08,306
and then purple,

358
00:11:08,306 --> 00:11:10,825
and the first streetlight buzzed on at

359
00:11:10,825 --> 00:11:12,217
the end of the block,

360
00:11:12,217 --> 00:11:14,802
you heard your mother calling your name

361
00:11:14,802 --> 00:11:16,127
from the back porch.

362
00:11:16,307 --> 00:11:17,653
And you rode home,

363
00:11:17,653 --> 00:11:19,596
tired and dirty and happy,

364
00:11:19,596 --> 00:11:21,988
as fast as your legs would carry

365
00:11:21,988 --> 00:11:22,286
you.

366
00:11:22,636 --> 00:11:23,653
In the evenings,

367
00:11:23,653 --> 00:11:25,241
and on Saturday mornings,

368
00:11:25,241 --> 00:11:27,782
the television became your window on the

369
00:11:27,782 --> 00:11:28,163
world.

370
00:11:28,343 --> 00:11:29,892
There was Howdy Doody,

371
00:11:29,892 --> 00:11:32,357
with his freckles and his big grin,

372
00:11:32,357 --> 00:11:33,484
and Buffalo Bob,

373
00:11:33,484 --> 00:11:36,019
and the Peanut Gallery full of lucky

374
00:11:36,019 --> 00:11:37,921
kids sitting in the studio.

375
00:11:38,270 --> 00:11:41,596
Then, in October of nineteen fifty-five,

376
00:11:41,596 --> 00:11:43,425
something new arrived.

377
00:11:43,605 --> 00:11:46,473
A show called the Mickey Mouse Club.

378
00:11:46,653 --> 00:11:48,882
Kids all over the country put on

379
00:11:48,882 --> 00:11:51,181
felt hats with big round ears and

380
00:11:51,181 --> 00:11:51,947
sang along.

381
00:11:52,127 --> 00:11:53,160
You know the song.

382
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:55,233
You can probably still hear it.

383
00:11:55,582 --> 00:11:57,064
That very same week,

384
00:11:57,064 --> 00:11:59,436
a gentle man with a bowl haircut

385
00:11:59,436 --> 00:12:02,474
and a coat with enormous pockets appeared

386
00:12:02,474 --> 00:12:03,141
on C B S.

387
00:12:03,321 --> 00:12:05,573
His name was Captain Kangaroo.

388
00:12:05,753 --> 00:12:07,510
He would still be there,

389
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:09,193
quietly, decades later,

390
00:12:09,193 --> 00:12:10,803
for your own children.

391
00:12:11,154 --> 00:12:13,285
Your parents watched I Love Lucy,

392
00:12:13,285 --> 00:12:15,159
and laughed until they cried.

393
00:12:15,339 --> 00:12:18,137
They watched Ed Sullivan on Sunday nights.

394
00:12:18,317 --> 00:12:21,355
And in September of nineteen fifty-five,

395
00:12:21,355 --> 00:12:24,772
a western called Gunsmoke came to television.

396
00:12:24,952 --> 00:12:26,868
It would run for twenty years.

397
00:12:27,218 --> 00:12:28,539
On Sunday evenings,

398
00:12:28,539 --> 00:12:31,112
the whole family gathered for Lassie,

399
00:12:31,112 --> 00:12:33,546
the collie who always found the boy

400
00:12:33,546 --> 00:12:34,102
in time.

401
00:12:34,282 --> 00:12:35,916
And when the show ended,

402
00:12:35,916 --> 00:12:37,074
it was bath time,

403
00:12:37,074 --> 00:12:39,729
and bedtime, and the quiet feeling that

404
00:12:39,729 --> 00:12:41,908
a new school week was waiting on

405
00:12:41,908 --> 00:12:43,814
the other side of the night.

406
00:12:43,994 --> 00:12:46,144
You fell asleep listening to the murmur

407
00:12:46,144 --> 00:12:48,734
of your parents' television through the bedroom

408
00:12:48,734 --> 00:12:49,010
wall.

409
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:50,846
On Saturday afternoons,

410
00:12:50,846 --> 00:12:52,657
there was the movie matinee.

411
00:12:52,837 --> 00:12:55,023
For a few coins you got a

412
00:12:55,023 --> 00:12:56,771
cartoon, a newsreel,

413
00:12:56,771 --> 00:12:58,608
a cliffhanger serial,

414
00:12:58,608 --> 00:12:59,832
and a feature,

415
00:12:59,832 --> 00:13:01,231
all in one long,

416
00:13:01,231 --> 00:13:04,029
loud, popcorn-scented afternoon.

417
00:13:04,209 --> 00:13:06,270
The theater was dark and cool,

418
00:13:06,270 --> 00:13:08,536
and the balcony was where the big

419
00:13:08,536 --> 00:13:09,155
kids sat.

420
00:13:09,504 --> 00:13:11,820
But the biggest change wasn't on the

421
00:13:11,820 --> 00:13:12,720
screen at all.

422
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:14,166
It was on the radio.

423
00:13:14,346 --> 00:13:17,037
In the summer of nineteen fifty-five,

424
00:13:17,037 --> 00:13:19,655
a song called Rock Around the Clock,

425
00:13:19,655 --> 00:13:21,765
by Bill Haley and His Comets,

426
00:13:21,765 --> 00:13:24,238
climbed all the way to number one.

427
00:13:24,418 --> 00:13:26,589
Teenagers went wild for it.

428
00:13:26,769 --> 00:13:28,452
Some parents were horrified.

429
00:13:28,802 --> 00:13:29,633
It was loud,

430
00:13:29,633 --> 00:13:30,739
and it was fast,

431
00:13:30,739 --> 00:13:33,230
and it seemed to come from somewhere

432
00:13:33,230 --> 00:13:35,443
the grown-ups didn't understand.

433
00:13:35,623 --> 00:13:36,875
You were only seven,

434
00:13:36,875 --> 00:13:37,876
but you noticed.

435
00:13:38,056 --> 00:13:40,320
Your older cousins combed their hair differently.

436
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:42,937
The big kids at the drugstore fed

437
00:13:42,937 --> 00:13:44,784
nickels into the jukebox.

438
00:13:45,134 --> 00:13:46,350
A few years later,

439
00:13:46,350 --> 00:13:48,512
the music went into your pocket.

440
00:13:48,692 --> 00:13:50,726
Small transistor radios,

441
00:13:50,726 --> 00:13:53,438
made of plastic and powered by a

442
00:13:53,438 --> 00:13:56,997
battery, let teenagers carry rock and roll

443
00:13:56,997 --> 00:13:57,759
anywhere.

444
00:13:57,939 --> 00:13:58,833
To the beach.

445
00:13:59,013 --> 00:14:00,151
To the backyard.

446
00:14:00,331 --> 00:14:01,958
Under the covers at night,

447
00:14:01,958 --> 00:14:04,086
with the volume turned way down so

448
00:14:04,086 --> 00:14:05,776
your parents wouldn't hear.

449
00:14:05,956 --> 00:14:07,386
For the first time,

450
00:14:07,386 --> 00:14:09,718
the music belonged to the kids.

451
00:14:10,068 --> 00:14:13,838
And then, in September of nineteen fifty-six,

452
00:14:13,838 --> 00:14:15,849
a young man from Tupelo,

453
00:14:15,849 --> 00:14:19,620
Mississippi, with long sideburns and a curled

454
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:22,804
lip, appeared on The Ed Sullivan Show.

455
00:14:22,984 --> 00:14:23,994
Elvis Presley.

456
00:14:24,174 --> 00:14:26,760
Around sixty million people watched,

457
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:28,771
by the most common estimate.

458
00:14:28,951 --> 00:14:31,250
Your parents watched with their arms folded.

459
00:14:31,430 --> 00:14:33,369
And you watched with your mouth open.

460
00:14:33,719 --> 00:14:35,448
The world was getting louder.

461
00:14:35,628 --> 00:14:36,929
And you were getting older.

462
00:14:37,279 --> 00:14:38,950
Now let's talk about money.

463
00:14:39,130 --> 00:14:41,006
Because if you grew up in nineteen

464
00:14:41,006 --> 00:14:44,150
fifty-five, you've probably told your grandchildren about

465
00:14:44,150 --> 00:14:46,467
the prices, and they've probably looked at

466
00:14:46,467 --> 00:14:48,012
you like you made it all up.

467
00:14:48,192 --> 00:14:48,726
You didn't.

468
00:14:49,076 --> 00:14:51,099
In the middle of the fifties,

469
00:14:51,099 --> 00:14:54,028
a gallon of gasoline cost somewhere around

470
00:14:54,028 --> 00:14:57,377
twenty-five cents, depending on where you lived.

471
00:14:57,557 --> 00:14:59,300
And a man in a uniform pumped

472
00:14:59,300 --> 00:14:59,961
it for you,

473
00:14:59,961 --> 00:15:01,343
washed your windshield,

474
00:15:01,343 --> 00:15:02,605
and checked your oil,

475
00:15:02,605 --> 00:15:04,708
without you ever leaving your seat.

476
00:15:05,059 --> 00:15:07,020
A loaf of bread was under a

477
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:07,601
quarter.

478
00:15:07,781 --> 00:15:10,637
A first-class stamp was three cents.

479
00:15:10,817 --> 00:15:13,016
A movie ticket was well under a

480
00:15:13,016 --> 00:15:15,286
dollar, and kids got in for even

481
00:15:15,286 --> 00:15:17,272
less on Saturday afternoons.

482
00:15:17,623 --> 00:15:19,163
A brand-new Chevrolet,

483
00:15:19,163 --> 00:15:22,385
the beautiful nineteen fifty-five Bel Air with

484
00:15:22,385 --> 00:15:23,995
its new V eight engine,

485
00:15:23,995 --> 00:15:26,376
cost roughly two thousand dollars.

486
00:15:26,556 --> 00:15:28,592
And a new house in the suburbs

487
00:15:28,592 --> 00:15:31,375
cost around ten thousand dollars or more.

488
00:15:31,725 --> 00:15:34,349
But here's the part the grandchildren always

489
00:15:34,349 --> 00:15:34,766
forget.

490
00:15:34,946 --> 00:15:36,390
Money was smaller,

491
00:15:36,390 --> 00:15:36,711
too.

492
00:15:36,891 --> 00:15:39,066
According to the Census Bureau,

493
00:15:39,066 --> 00:15:42,294
the typical American family earned around four

494
00:15:42,294 --> 00:15:45,101
thousand, four hundred dollars a year in

495
00:15:45,101 --> 00:15:46,504
nineteen fifty-five.

496
00:15:46,684 --> 00:15:49,718
The federal minimum wage was seventy-five cents

497
00:15:49,718 --> 00:15:51,396
an hour, and it went up to

498
00:15:51,396 --> 00:15:53,720
a whole dollar the following spring.

499
00:15:54,070 --> 00:15:56,841
So when your father bought that Chevrolet,

500
00:15:56,841 --> 00:15:58,623
it was a very big decision,

501
00:15:58,623 --> 00:16:00,999
talked over at the kitchen table for

502
00:16:00,999 --> 00:16:01,395
weeks.

503
00:16:01,575 --> 00:16:04,118
People paid cash for most things.

504
00:16:04,298 --> 00:16:05,296
They saved up.

505
00:16:05,476 --> 00:16:07,836
A family might have one car,

506
00:16:07,836 --> 00:16:10,701
one television, and one telephone.

507
00:16:11,051 --> 00:16:13,628
The telephone was mounted on the wall,

508
00:16:13,628 --> 00:16:15,460
or sat on a little table in

509
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:17,902
the hallway, with a rotary dial that

510
00:16:17,902 --> 00:16:20,547
clicked back around after every number.

511
00:16:20,727 --> 00:16:22,123
And if you were unlucky,

512
00:16:22,123 --> 00:16:23,286
it was a party line,

513
00:16:23,286 --> 00:16:25,438
where the neighbors could pick up and

514
00:16:25,438 --> 00:16:27,241
listen in on your conversation.

515
00:16:27,421 --> 00:16:28,557
On a party line,

516
00:16:28,557 --> 00:16:31,113
everybody knew everybody's business.

517
00:16:31,463 --> 00:16:33,743
If you needed to call your grandmother

518
00:16:33,743 --> 00:16:34,763
in another state,

519
00:16:34,763 --> 00:16:35,723
it was an event.

520
00:16:35,903 --> 00:16:38,045
Long distance was expensive,

521
00:16:38,045 --> 00:16:40,951
so the whole family crowded around the

522
00:16:40,951 --> 00:16:43,475
phone, each person taking a turn,

523
00:16:43,475 --> 00:16:46,610
talking fast, and somebody always saying,

524
00:16:46,610 --> 00:16:49,058
hurry up, this is costing money.

525
00:16:49,238 --> 00:16:50,360
Most of the time,

526
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:52,209
people simply wrote letters,

527
00:16:52,209 --> 00:16:54,323
and waited a week for the reply.

528
00:16:54,673 --> 00:16:56,836
Your allowance might have been a quarter

529
00:16:56,836 --> 00:16:57,215
a week.

530
00:16:57,395 --> 00:16:58,686
Maybe fifty cents,

531
00:16:58,686 --> 00:17:00,263
if you mowed the lawn.

532
00:17:00,443 --> 00:17:02,387
And a quarter could buy you a

533
00:17:02,387 --> 00:17:03,660
comic book, a soda,

534
00:17:03,660 --> 00:17:04,465
a candy bar,

535
00:17:04,465 --> 00:17:06,811
and still leave a little change for

536
00:17:06,811 --> 00:17:08,152
the gumball machine.

537
00:17:08,502 --> 00:17:10,046
You didn't feel rich.

538
00:17:10,226 --> 00:17:11,781
Most families didn't.

539
00:17:11,961 --> 00:17:14,220
But you felt like things were getting

540
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:15,746
better every single year.

541
00:17:15,926 --> 00:17:18,464
And for millions of American families in

542
00:17:18,464 --> 00:17:19,796
the nineteen fifties,

543
00:17:19,796 --> 00:17:20,431
they were.

544
00:17:20,781 --> 00:17:22,990
Some days in the year were different

545
00:17:22,990 --> 00:17:24,217
from all the others.

546
00:17:24,397 --> 00:17:26,385
Summer meant no school,

547
00:17:26,385 --> 00:17:27,509
and no shoes.

548
00:17:27,689 --> 00:17:29,768
By July, the bottoms of your feet

549
00:17:29,768 --> 00:17:31,911
were so tough you could run across

550
00:17:31,911 --> 00:17:33,865
hot pavement without flinching.

551
00:17:34,045 --> 00:17:36,487
You caught fireflies in a mason jar

552
00:17:36,487 --> 00:17:38,581
with holes punched in the lid,

553
00:17:38,581 --> 00:17:40,605
and set them free before bed.

554
00:17:40,785 --> 00:17:43,355
Or forgot, and felt terrible in the

555
00:17:43,355 --> 00:17:43,943
morning.

556
00:17:44,293 --> 00:17:46,301
You ran through the lawn sprinkler.

557
00:17:46,481 --> 00:17:48,798
You waited, every single afternoon,

558
00:17:48,798 --> 00:17:50,717
for the bell of the ice cream

559
00:17:50,717 --> 00:17:52,967
truck, and when you heard it three

560
00:17:52,967 --> 00:17:56,210
blocks away, the whole neighborhood came running.

561
00:17:56,561 --> 00:17:58,054
On the Fourth of July,

562
00:17:58,054 --> 00:18:00,498
there was a parade down Main Street,

563
00:18:00,498 --> 00:18:02,738
with the high school band and the

564
00:18:02,738 --> 00:18:05,454
veterans marching in their old uniforms.

565
00:18:05,634 --> 00:18:07,053
There was a picnic,

566
00:18:07,053 --> 00:18:09,517
with potato salad and watermelon,

567
00:18:09,517 --> 00:18:12,279
and somebody's uncle in charge of the

568
00:18:12,279 --> 00:18:12,727
grill.

569
00:18:12,907 --> 00:18:14,331
And when it got dark,

570
00:18:14,331 --> 00:18:16,500
you sat on a blanket and watched

571
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:19,212
the fireworks bloom over the ball field.

572
00:18:19,562 --> 00:18:21,152
Sundays were quieter.

573
00:18:21,332 --> 00:18:22,969
Most stores were closed.

574
00:18:23,149 --> 00:18:25,525
Many families went to church in their

575
00:18:25,525 --> 00:18:28,028
best clothes, the girls in white gloves

576
00:18:28,028 --> 00:18:30,147
and the boys in clip-on ties that

577
00:18:30,147 --> 00:18:30,661
pinched.

578
00:18:30,841 --> 00:18:32,432
Then came Sunday dinner,

579
00:18:32,432 --> 00:18:34,355
the biggest meal of the week,

580
00:18:34,355 --> 00:18:36,411
maybe a pot roast that had been

581
00:18:36,411 --> 00:18:37,737
cooking all morning.

582
00:18:38,087 --> 00:18:39,600
And in the afternoon,

583
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:40,176
a drive.

584
00:18:40,356 --> 00:18:41,459
Just a drive.

585
00:18:41,639 --> 00:18:43,169
Your father would say,

586
00:18:43,169 --> 00:18:44,560
let's go for a ride,

587
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:47,063
and the whole family would pile into

588
00:18:47,063 --> 00:18:49,635
the car and go nowhere in particular,

589
00:18:49,635 --> 00:18:51,443
just to look at the farms,

590
00:18:51,443 --> 00:18:53,320
or the new houses going up,

591
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:54,155
or the lake.

592
00:18:54,505 --> 00:18:55,921
And then there was Christmas.

593
00:18:56,101 --> 00:18:58,892
The big department store catalog arrived in

594
00:18:58,892 --> 00:19:00,903
the fall, and you read it until

595
00:19:00,903 --> 00:19:02,201
the pages went soft,

596
00:19:02,201 --> 00:19:05,122
circling everything you wanted with a pencil.

597
00:19:05,302 --> 00:19:06,486
An electric train.

598
00:19:06,666 --> 00:19:07,850
A baby doll.

599
00:19:08,030 --> 00:19:09,065
A real air rifle,

600
00:19:09,065 --> 00:19:11,194
if your mother could be talked into

601
00:19:11,194 --> 00:19:13,081
it, which she usually couldn't.

602
00:19:13,431 --> 00:19:15,314
The tree was a real one,

603
00:19:15,314 --> 00:19:17,198
and the lights were big,

604
00:19:17,198 --> 00:19:18,690
hot, colored bulbs.

605
00:19:18,870 --> 00:19:20,278
If one bulb went out,

606
00:19:20,278 --> 00:19:22,760
sometimes the whole string went dark,

607
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:25,242
and your father spent an hour testing

608
00:19:25,242 --> 00:19:26,382
every single one,

609
00:19:26,382 --> 00:19:28,193
grumbling under his breath.

610
00:19:28,373 --> 00:19:30,638
You threw the tinsel on in clumps,

611
00:19:30,638 --> 00:19:32,971
and your mother came behind you and

612
00:19:32,971 --> 00:19:35,037
fixed it, one strand at a time.

613
00:19:35,387 --> 00:19:36,521
On Christmas Eve,

614
00:19:36,521 --> 00:19:38,057
you could hardly sleep.

615
00:19:38,237 --> 00:19:39,940
And on Christmas morning,

616
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:41,031
in your pajamas,

617
00:19:41,031 --> 00:19:43,075
in the gray light before dawn,

618
00:19:43,075 --> 00:19:45,323
you crept down the stairs and saw

619
00:19:45,323 --> 00:19:45,528
it.

620
00:19:45,708 --> 00:19:46,730
The tree lit up.

621
00:19:46,910 --> 00:19:48,349
The presents underneath.

622
00:19:48,529 --> 00:19:49,658
And for a moment,

623
00:19:49,658 --> 00:19:52,248
everything in the world was exactly the

624
00:19:52,248 --> 00:19:53,974
way it was supposed to be.

625
00:19:54,325 --> 00:19:57,077
There's one thing about nineteen fifty-five that

626
00:19:57,077 --> 00:19:59,371
people who weren't there can never quite

627
00:19:59,371 --> 00:20:00,002
understand.

628
00:20:00,182 --> 00:20:01,382
Before that year,

629
00:20:01,382 --> 00:20:04,419
every summer, American parents were afraid.

630
00:20:04,599 --> 00:20:06,306
They were afraid of polio.

631
00:20:06,657 --> 00:20:08,921
Polio came in the warm months.

632
00:20:09,101 --> 00:20:12,456
It paralyzed thousands of children every year.

633
00:20:12,636 --> 00:20:14,424
Swimming pools closed.

634
00:20:14,604 --> 00:20:17,399
Parents kept their kids away from crowds,

635
00:20:17,399 --> 00:20:19,103
away from movie theaters,

636
00:20:19,103 --> 00:20:20,467
away from the beach.

637
00:20:20,647 --> 00:20:23,294
Some children ended up in leg braces.

638
00:20:23,474 --> 00:20:25,454
Some ended up in iron lungs,

639
00:20:25,454 --> 00:20:28,495
huge metal machines that breathed for them,

640
00:20:28,495 --> 00:20:30,405
lined up in hospital wards.

641
00:20:30,755 --> 00:20:32,970
Every mother in America knew the fear

642
00:20:32,970 --> 00:20:34,587
of a child who woke up with

643
00:20:34,587 --> 00:20:36,084
a stiff neck and a fever.

644
00:20:36,264 --> 00:20:38,136
Then, on April twelfth,

645
00:20:38,136 --> 00:20:41,638
nineteen fifty-five, the news came over the

646
00:20:41,638 --> 00:20:42,127
radio.

647
00:20:42,307 --> 00:20:45,247
The vaccine developed by Doctor Jonas Salk

648
00:20:45,247 --> 00:20:46,927
was safe, and it worked.

649
00:20:47,278 --> 00:20:48,589
Church bells rang.

650
00:20:48,769 --> 00:20:50,569
People cried in the streets.

651
00:20:50,749 --> 00:20:52,834
And soon you were standing in a

652
00:20:52,834 --> 00:20:54,178
long line at school,

653
00:20:54,178 --> 00:20:55,523
or in the gymnasium,

654
00:20:55,523 --> 00:20:57,070
rolling up your sleeve,

655
00:20:57,070 --> 00:20:59,020
trying very hard to be brave,

656
00:20:59,020 --> 00:21:01,104
for a shot that meant you would

657
00:21:01,104 --> 00:21:03,256
never have to be afraid of polio

658
00:21:03,256 --> 00:21:03,659
again.

659
00:21:03,839 --> 00:21:05,819
A lot of kids remember that shot

660
00:21:05,819 --> 00:21:08,480
more clearly than almost anything else from

661
00:21:08,480 --> 00:21:09,099
that year.

662
00:21:09,279 --> 00:21:10,463
Maybe you do too.

663
00:21:10,813 --> 00:21:12,134
That same summer,

664
00:21:12,134 --> 00:21:13,690
on July seventeenth,

665
00:21:13,690 --> 00:21:16,645
something else opened its gates out in

666
00:21:16,645 --> 00:21:17,500
California.

667
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:19,712
A place called Disneyland.

668
00:21:19,892 --> 00:21:21,861
Most families couldn't go,

669
00:21:21,861 --> 00:21:22,922
not that year,

670
00:21:22,922 --> 00:21:24,361
and maybe not ever.

671
00:21:24,541 --> 00:21:26,431
But you saw it on television,

672
00:21:26,431 --> 00:21:28,060
and you dreamed about it,

673
00:21:28,060 --> 00:21:30,601
and for a whole generation of children,

674
00:21:30,601 --> 00:21:32,947
it felt like the future had arrived.

675
00:21:33,297 --> 00:21:35,636
There were other things happening too,

676
00:21:35,636 --> 00:21:38,776
things a seven-year-old might not fully understand.

677
00:21:38,956 --> 00:21:40,032
In Des Plaines,

678
00:21:40,032 --> 00:21:43,045
Illinois, a salesman named Ray Kroc opened

679
00:21:43,045 --> 00:21:46,201
his first McDonald's restaurant that spring.

680
00:21:46,551 --> 00:21:47,783
And in December,

681
00:21:47,783 --> 00:21:49,555
in Montgomery, Alabama,

682
00:21:49,555 --> 00:21:52,251
a woman named Rosa Parks refused to

683
00:21:52,251 --> 00:21:54,253
give up her seat on a city

684
00:21:54,253 --> 00:21:54,561
bus.

685
00:21:54,741 --> 00:21:56,915
The grown-ups talked about that one in

686
00:21:56,915 --> 00:21:58,689
low voices at the dinner table.

687
00:21:58,869 --> 00:22:00,924
It would take you years to understand

688
00:22:00,924 --> 00:22:03,035
just how much that moment would change

689
00:22:03,035 --> 00:22:03,757
your country.

690
00:22:04,107 --> 00:22:06,240
And then the years began to move

691
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:06,707
faster.

692
00:22:06,887 --> 00:22:08,417
In nineteen fifty-six,

693
00:22:08,417 --> 00:22:11,755
President Eisenhower signed the law that created

694
00:22:11,755 --> 00:22:13,842
the Interstate Highway System.

695
00:22:14,022 --> 00:22:16,851
Soon your family was taking road trips

696
00:22:16,851 --> 00:22:18,191
on wide new roads,

697
00:22:18,191 --> 00:22:21,318
stopping at motels with swimming pools and

698
00:22:21,318 --> 00:22:22,137
neon signs.

699
00:22:22,487 --> 00:22:24,300
You sat in the back seat,

700
00:22:24,300 --> 00:22:25,606
with no seat belt,

701
00:22:25,606 --> 00:22:27,855
playing the license plate game,

702
00:22:27,855 --> 00:22:29,596
asking are we there yet,

703
00:22:29,596 --> 00:22:32,497
until your father threatened to turn the

704
00:22:32,497 --> 00:22:33,295
car around.

705
00:22:33,475 --> 00:22:35,346
And on summer nights you went to

706
00:22:35,346 --> 00:22:37,040
the drive-in in your pajamas,

707
00:22:37,040 --> 00:22:38,910
and fell asleep in the back seat

708
00:22:38,910 --> 00:22:40,430
before the second feature.

709
00:22:40,780 --> 00:22:43,212
In October of nineteen fifty-seven,

710
00:22:43,212 --> 00:22:44,184
you were nine,

711
00:22:44,184 --> 00:22:46,686
and one evening your father took you

712
00:22:46,686 --> 00:22:49,187
out into the backyard and pointed up

713
00:22:49,187 --> 00:22:49,951
at the sky.

714
00:22:50,131 --> 00:22:51,453
Somewhere up there,

715
00:22:51,453 --> 00:22:54,164
a little silver ball called Sputnik was

716
00:22:54,164 --> 00:22:56,459
beeping its way around the Earth.

717
00:22:56,639 --> 00:22:58,427
The Soviets had put it there.

718
00:22:58,607 --> 00:22:59,861
And the grown-ups,

719
00:22:59,861 --> 00:23:02,438
who had seemed so sure of everything,

720
00:23:02,438 --> 00:23:04,110
suddenly looked worried.

721
00:23:04,460 --> 00:23:05,435
Within a year,

722
00:23:05,435 --> 00:23:08,291
your school was talking about science and

723
00:23:08,291 --> 00:23:10,381
mathematics like never before.

724
00:23:10,561 --> 00:23:12,150
The space race had begun,

725
00:23:12,150 --> 00:23:14,185
and you were right in the middle

726
00:23:14,185 --> 00:23:14,566
of it.

727
00:23:14,916 --> 00:23:16,570
In nineteen fifty-nine,

728
00:23:16,570 --> 00:23:17,720
you were eleven,

729
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:19,734
and a new decade was coming.

730
00:23:19,914 --> 00:23:23,265
Alaska and Hawaii became the forty-ninth and

731
00:23:23,265 --> 00:23:26,008
fiftieth states, and soon a new flag

732
00:23:26,008 --> 00:23:28,902
with fifty stars hung on the classroom

733
00:23:28,902 --> 00:23:29,283
wall.

734
00:23:29,463 --> 00:23:32,876
Your father's car grew enormous tail fins.

735
00:23:33,056 --> 00:23:35,809
Everything seemed to point toward the sky,

736
00:23:35,809 --> 00:23:37,120
and toward tomorrow.

737
00:23:37,470 --> 00:23:38,694
In nineteen sixty,

738
00:23:38,694 --> 00:23:39,782
you were twelve,

739
00:23:39,782 --> 00:23:42,299
and you watched a young senator named

740
00:23:42,299 --> 00:23:42,775
John F.

741
00:23:42,955 --> 00:23:45,997
Kennedy debate Richard Nixon on television.

742
00:23:46,177 --> 00:23:49,184
It was the first televised presidential debate.

743
00:23:49,364 --> 00:23:51,199
Kennedy won the election.

744
00:23:51,379 --> 00:23:52,598
He seemed so young.

745
00:23:52,778 --> 00:23:54,925
He made the future sound exciting.

746
00:23:55,276 --> 00:23:57,043
Your body was changing,

747
00:23:57,043 --> 00:23:59,119
and so was everything else.

748
00:23:59,299 --> 00:24:02,123
Junior high meant lockers and changing classes

749
00:24:02,123 --> 00:24:04,088
and the terrifying school dance,

750
00:24:04,088 --> 00:24:06,053
where the boys stood on one side

751
00:24:06,053 --> 00:24:07,895
of the gym and the girls stood

752
00:24:07,895 --> 00:24:08,693
on the other,

753
00:24:08,693 --> 00:24:11,210
and the chaperones watched from the door.

754
00:24:11,390 --> 00:24:13,898
Somebody finally crossed the floor.

755
00:24:14,078 --> 00:24:15,262
Maybe it was you.

756
00:24:15,613 --> 00:24:18,018
In October of nineteen sixty-two,

757
00:24:18,018 --> 00:24:19,330
you were fourteen,

758
00:24:19,330 --> 00:24:21,298
and for thirteen long days,

759
00:24:21,298 --> 00:24:24,068
the whole country held its breath over

760
00:24:24,068 --> 00:24:25,307
missiles in Cuba.

761
00:24:25,487 --> 00:24:28,857
Those duck and cover drills suddenly didn't

762
00:24:28,857 --> 00:24:30,816
feel like a game anymore.

763
00:24:31,166 --> 00:24:33,825
And then came November twenty-second,

764
00:24:33,825 --> 00:24:35,334
nineteen sixty-three.

765
00:24:35,514 --> 00:24:36,617
You were fifteen.

766
00:24:36,797 --> 00:24:40,486
Almost everyone your age remembers exactly where

767
00:24:40,486 --> 00:24:41,255
they were.

768
00:24:41,435 --> 00:24:43,326
A teacher came into the room,

769
00:24:43,326 --> 00:24:45,805
or the principal's voice came over the

770
00:24:45,805 --> 00:24:48,740
loudspeaker, and you heard that the president

771
00:24:48,740 --> 00:24:50,305
had been shot in Dallas.

772
00:24:50,485 --> 00:24:52,041
School let out early.

773
00:24:52,391 --> 00:24:55,408
At home, the television stayed on for

774
00:24:55,408 --> 00:24:55,816
days.

775
00:24:55,996 --> 00:24:57,801
It was the first time many of

776
00:24:57,801 --> 00:24:59,606
you ever saw your father cry.

777
00:24:59,957 --> 00:25:01,770
But even in that dark winter,

778
00:25:01,770 --> 00:25:03,521
something bright was coming.

779
00:25:03,701 --> 00:25:05,121
On February ninth,

780
00:25:05,121 --> 00:25:09,067
nineteen sixty-four, four young men from Liverpool

781
00:25:09,067 --> 00:25:11,513
walked onto the stage of The Ed

782
00:25:11,513 --> 00:25:12,617
Sullivan Show.

783
00:25:12,797 --> 00:25:13,680
The Beatles.

784
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:17,691
Around seventy-three million Americans watched that night.

785
00:25:18,041 --> 00:25:20,028
If you were fifteen or sixteen,

786
00:25:20,028 --> 00:25:21,759
you remember the screaming,

787
00:25:21,759 --> 00:25:24,131
and your parents shaking their heads,

788
00:25:24,131 --> 00:25:26,311
and the feeling deep in your chest

789
00:25:26,311 --> 00:25:29,196
that your generation finally had something of

790
00:25:29,196 --> 00:25:29,709
its own.

791
00:25:30,059 --> 00:25:31,538
And now we come to the end

792
00:25:31,538 --> 00:25:32,392
of our journey.

793
00:25:32,572 --> 00:25:34,866
Or really, to the beginning of a

794
00:25:34,866 --> 00:25:35,440
new one.

795
00:25:35,620 --> 00:25:37,251
When you turned sixteen,

796
00:25:37,251 --> 00:25:39,696
sometime around nineteen sixty-four,

797
00:25:39,696 --> 00:25:42,550
one thing mattered more than anything else

798
00:25:42,550 --> 00:25:43,433
in the world.

799
00:25:43,613 --> 00:25:45,320
Your driver's license.

800
00:25:45,670 --> 00:25:48,676
Maybe you took driver's education at school,

801
00:25:48,676 --> 00:25:50,590
in a car with an extra brake

802
00:25:50,590 --> 00:25:52,981
pedal on the passenger side for the

803
00:25:52,981 --> 00:25:56,261
instructor, who gripped the dashboard every time

804
00:25:56,261 --> 00:25:57,628
you turned a corner.

805
00:25:57,808 --> 00:26:00,030
Maybe your father taught you,

806
00:26:00,030 --> 00:26:02,405
out on an empty country road on

807
00:26:02,405 --> 00:26:03,861
a Sunday afternoon,

808
00:26:03,861 --> 00:26:06,313
telling you to watch the clutch,

809
00:26:06,313 --> 00:26:07,616
watch the clutch,

810
00:26:07,616 --> 00:26:08,919
watch the clutch.

811
00:26:09,269 --> 00:26:11,242
You stalled it a dozen times.

812
00:26:11,422 --> 00:26:12,979
And then, one day,

813
00:26:12,979 --> 00:26:13,930
you didn't.

814
00:26:14,110 --> 00:26:15,561
And then came the car.

815
00:26:15,741 --> 00:26:17,959
Very few kids got a new one.

816
00:26:18,139 --> 00:26:20,392
In April of nineteen sixty-four,

817
00:26:20,392 --> 00:26:22,363
Ford introduced the Mustang,

818
00:26:22,363 --> 00:26:24,334
at a base price of about two

819
00:26:24,334 --> 00:26:27,431
thousand, three hundred sixty-eight dollars,

820
00:26:27,431 --> 00:26:30,318
and every teenager in America wanted one.

821
00:26:30,498 --> 00:26:32,704
But you probably didn't get a Mustang.

822
00:26:33,054 --> 00:26:34,505
You got something used.

823
00:26:34,685 --> 00:26:36,712
Something with a few dents,

824
00:26:36,712 --> 00:26:39,040
and a radio that only pulled in

825
00:26:39,040 --> 00:26:41,443
two stations, and a smell of old

826
00:26:41,443 --> 00:26:44,522
cigarettes and motor oil that never quite

827
00:26:44,522 --> 00:26:45,273
went away.

828
00:26:45,453 --> 00:26:47,480
Maybe it was a fifty-five Chevy,

829
00:26:47,480 --> 00:26:49,696
the same model your father had been

830
00:26:49,696 --> 00:26:51,723
so proud of when you were seven.

831
00:26:51,903 --> 00:26:53,700
Now it was nine years old,

832
00:26:53,700 --> 00:26:54,875
and it was yours.

833
00:26:55,225 --> 00:26:57,399
You paid for it with money you

834
00:26:57,399 --> 00:26:59,937
saved yourself, from a paper route,

835
00:26:59,937 --> 00:27:01,459
or bagging groceries,

836
00:27:01,459 --> 00:27:04,141
or pumping gas at the corner station.

837
00:27:04,321 --> 00:27:06,150
Every dollar of it was yours,

838
00:27:06,150 --> 00:27:08,106
and that made the car feel like

839
00:27:08,106 --> 00:27:10,440
the most valuable thing in the world.

840
00:27:10,790 --> 00:27:12,973
The first time you drove alone,

841
00:27:12,973 --> 00:27:15,368
with nobody in the passenger seat,

842
00:27:15,368 --> 00:27:17,129
you went nowhere special.

843
00:27:17,309 --> 00:27:19,214
Maybe just around the block,

844
00:27:19,214 --> 00:27:20,710
and then around again.

845
00:27:20,890 --> 00:27:22,836
But you rolled the window down,

846
00:27:22,836 --> 00:27:24,593
and you felt the air on your

847
00:27:24,593 --> 00:27:27,416
arm, and you understood something that nobody

848
00:27:27,416 --> 00:27:28,797
had to explain to you.

849
00:27:28,977 --> 00:27:29,859
You were grown.

850
00:27:30,039 --> 00:27:31,363
Or close enough.

851
00:27:31,713 --> 00:27:33,512
You washed it every Saturday.

852
00:27:33,692 --> 00:27:36,130
You knew every rattle and every squeak.

853
00:27:36,310 --> 00:27:38,882
You drove your friends to the drive-in,

854
00:27:38,882 --> 00:27:40,465
and to the burger stand,

855
00:27:40,465 --> 00:27:42,839
and around and around Main Street on

856
00:27:42,839 --> 00:27:45,345
Friday night with the windows down and

857
00:27:45,345 --> 00:27:46,202
the radio up.

858
00:27:46,552 --> 00:27:48,396
At the drive-in restaurant,

859
00:27:48,396 --> 00:27:50,650
a carhop came out to your window,

860
00:27:50,650 --> 00:27:52,494
sometimes on roller skates,

861
00:27:52,494 --> 00:27:54,407
and hung a tray right on the

862
00:27:54,407 --> 00:27:54,748
door.

863
00:27:54,928 --> 00:27:57,064
A burger, fries and a shake,

864
00:27:57,064 --> 00:27:59,506
and all your friends in the next

865
00:27:59,506 --> 00:28:01,871
car over, honking and laughing.

866
00:28:02,051 --> 00:28:04,344
Gas was still around thirty cents a

867
00:28:04,344 --> 00:28:06,767
gallon, so you could cruise all night

868
00:28:06,767 --> 00:28:08,274
on a couple of dollars.

869
00:28:08,624 --> 00:28:11,079
Maybe that car carried you to your

870
00:28:11,079 --> 00:28:12,234
first real date.

871
00:28:12,414 --> 00:28:14,440
You picked her up at her front

872
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:16,735
door, and shook her father's hand,

873
00:28:16,735 --> 00:28:18,896
and promised to have her home by

874
00:28:18,896 --> 00:28:19,369
eleven.

875
00:28:19,549 --> 00:28:21,348
Maybe you kept that promise.

876
00:28:21,528 --> 00:28:22,631
Maybe you didn't.

877
00:28:22,811 --> 00:28:24,947
Either way, you remember that night.

878
00:28:25,298 --> 00:28:26,728
That car was freedom,

879
00:28:26,728 --> 00:28:28,839
just like the bicycle had been.

880
00:28:29,019 --> 00:28:32,211
Only now, the neighborhood wasn't big enough

881
00:28:32,211 --> 00:28:32,792
anymore.

882
00:28:32,972 --> 00:28:35,421
Now the road went all the way

883
00:28:35,421 --> 00:28:36,687
to the horizon.

884
00:28:37,037 --> 00:28:38,569
So that was the journey.

885
00:28:38,749 --> 00:28:41,138
From a red bicycle leaning against a

886
00:28:41,138 --> 00:28:43,061
porch in nineteen fifty-five,

887
00:28:43,061 --> 00:28:44,919
to a used car humming in the

888
00:28:44,919 --> 00:28:46,644
driveway nine years later.

889
00:28:46,824 --> 00:28:47,806
In those years,

890
00:28:47,806 --> 00:28:49,900
you went from Howdy Doody to the

891
00:28:49,900 --> 00:28:50,423
Beatles.

892
00:28:50,603 --> 00:28:52,971
From the milkman to the supermarket.

893
00:28:53,151 --> 00:28:55,883
From a world where polio terrified every

894
00:28:55,883 --> 00:28:58,614
parent, to a world where children rolled

895
00:28:58,614 --> 00:29:01,072
up their sleeves and the fear simply

896
00:29:01,072 --> 00:29:01,754
went away.

897
00:29:02,105 --> 00:29:03,834
You hid under your desk,

898
00:29:03,834 --> 00:29:06,716
watched a satellite cross the night sky,

899
00:29:06,716 --> 00:29:09,094
and saw a president laid to rest.

900
00:29:09,274 --> 00:29:10,746
And through all of it,

901
00:29:10,746 --> 00:29:11,549
you grew up.

902
00:29:11,729 --> 00:29:13,750
The world you grew up in is

903
00:29:13,750 --> 00:29:14,423
gone now.

904
00:29:14,603 --> 00:29:16,832
The milkman doesn't come anymore.

905
00:29:17,012 --> 00:29:19,562
The screen doors don't slam the same

906
00:29:19,562 --> 00:29:19,845
way.

907
00:29:20,025 --> 00:29:22,303
Nobody calls the kids home when the

908
00:29:22,303 --> 00:29:23,670
streetlights come on.

909
00:29:24,021 --> 00:29:25,097
But if you lived it,

910
00:29:25,097 --> 00:29:26,389
you carried it with you.

911
00:29:26,569 --> 00:29:27,649
Every bit of it.

912
00:29:27,829 --> 00:29:29,814
The smell of your mother's kitchen.

913
00:29:29,994 --> 00:29:32,459
The clatter of that playing card in

914
00:29:32,459 --> 00:29:33,233
the spokes.

915
00:29:33,413 --> 00:29:35,006
The sound of the engine the first

916
00:29:35,006 --> 00:29:36,165
time you turned the key.

917
00:29:36,345 --> 00:29:38,110
That world is still there.

918
00:29:38,290 --> 00:29:39,149
It's in you.

919
00:29:39,499 --> 00:29:41,774
Now I'd love to hear from you.

920
00:29:41,954 --> 00:29:43,478
In the comments below,

921
00:29:43,478 --> 00:29:45,764
tell me what year you turned ten,

922
00:29:45,764 --> 00:29:48,049
and what your very first car was.

923
00:29:48,229 --> 00:29:49,840
I read every one of them,

924
00:29:49,840 --> 00:29:52,160
and some of your stories will become

925
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:54,092
future videos on this channel.

926
00:29:54,443 --> 00:29:56,725
And if this brought back memories,

927
00:29:56,725 --> 00:29:58,738
you'll enjoy our next journey.

928
00:29:58,918 --> 00:30:01,788
Because if you turned eighteen in nineteen

929
00:30:01,788 --> 00:30:04,726
fifty-seven, your story is waiting for you,

930
00:30:04,726 --> 00:30:05,478
right here.

931
00:30:05,658 --> 00:30:07,355
Thank you for spending this time with

932
00:30:07,355 --> 00:30:07,492
me.

933
00:30:07,672 --> 00:30:08,802
Until next time,

934
00:30:08,802 --> 00:30:10,354
